,title,link,text,language,family
0,Nares-jux - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nares-jux,"October 25, 2025 - The Ostyak (Khanty people) term the instrument nares-jux, meaning ""musical wood"" or ""singing tree"" in the Khanty language. The same instrument is played by the Mansi people (formerly known as Vogul), and is known as sangkultap (or са̄ӈквылтап (sāŋkwyltap)) in the Mansi language.",Mansi,Uralic
1,Mansi people - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansi_people,"2 weeks ago - The ancestors of the Mansi populated the areas west of the Urals. Mansi findings have been unearthed in the vicinity of Perm. In the first millennium BC, they expanded to Western Siberia, where they assimilated with the native inhabitants.",Mansi,Uralic
2,Shamanism in Siberia - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shamanism_in_Siberia,"February 26, 2026 - Mihály Hoppál continued Diószegi Vilmos's work comparing shamanic beliefs of speakers of Uralic languages with those of several non-Uralic Siberian peoples. Although Ugrian folklore preserves many traces of shamanism, shamanism itself was a dying practice among the Khanty and Mansi people ...",Mansi,Uralic
3,Mansi - Fenno-Ugria,https://fennougria.ee/en/peoples/mansis/,"June 14, 2023 - Names Autonyms of the Mansi are ‘maańśi’, ‘maańśi’, ‘maahum’, ‘mäńś’. Their neighbours the Khanty and Komi-Zyrians call them “Voguls” which through the Russian language has …",Mansi,Uralic
4,"Khanty and Mansi | History, Culture & Language | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/topic/Khanty,"April 17, 2026 - Of all the peoples of northern Siberia, only the Khanty and Mansi had stringed instruments: a type of five-stringed zither and a one- or two-stringed, bowed instrument (its bow made like a small archer’s bow strung with horsehair).",Mansi,Uralic
5,Mansi people Facts For Kids,https://www.diy.org/article/mansi_people,"The Mansi enjoy various festivals to celebrate their culture! 🎉One popular festival is called ""Khanty-Mansi Festival,"" where they gather to enjoy traditional music, dance, and food. 🥟Dancing and storytelling are important parts of the celebrations. Their food is made from local ingredients like fish, meat, and berries.",Mansi,Uralic
6,Uralic Songs - László Reynolds - Bandcamp,https://laszloreynolds.bandcamp.com/album/uralic-songs,"December 10, 2022 - released June 19, 2022 featuring guest musicians: Jong-Yun Lee - clarinet (1) Nicholas Tapsell - cello (1, 3) produced by László Reynolds and Seamus Maguire recorded and mixed by Seamus Maguire at Spektic Studios mastered by Ike Zwanikken ...",Mansi,Uralic
7,Mansi people — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Mansi_people,"January 14, 2026 - Beadwork and embroidery served both utilitarian and symbolic purposes, with silver jewelry such as necklaces and chest pieces incorporating intricate designs integral to ceremonial dress.[59][61] Performing arts encompassed ritual dances and music tied to festivals, notably the bear ceremony featuring songs like Kurenya and reenactments of hunting scenes to honor ancestral spirits. These practices preserved oral and kinesthetic traditions, with applied decorative arts supporting cultural identity through ornamented tools and ritual covers.[59][62] The Y-chromosome haplogroups of the Mansi population are dominated by subclades of haplogroup N, which are characteristic of Uralic-speaking groups across northern Eurasia, alongside contributions from R1a.",Mansi,Uralic
8,Face Music - Switzerland - People of Siberia - northern peoples - in English,http://www.face-music.ch/peopleofsiberia/northernpeoples_en.html,P & C December 1998 - Face Music / Albi · These small northern peoples pertain to various language families and were initially settling farther south:,Mansi,Uralic
9,Mansi | people | Britannica,https://www.britannica.com/topic/Mansi,"6 days ago - Mansi, western Siberian peoples, living mainly in the Ob River basin of central Russia. They each speak an Ob-Ugric language of the Finno-Ugric branch of the Uralic languages. Together they numbered some 30,000 in the late 20th century. They are descended from people from the…",Mansi,Uralic
